package errors

import text from "std/text"

// Package errors pairs an error with a message made at run time. A
// program imports it as
//
//     import errors from "std/errors"
//
// and the compiler reads it from $TACK_HOME/src/std/errors, or
// ~/.tack/src/std/errors, as it reads std/text. It is written in Tack
// alone, with no C.
//
// An error's message is a literal, fixed when the program is compiled, so
// what only the run knows, the name that was undefined or the path that
// could not be opened, has no place in it. A Failure holds both: the
// error, its code and its literal message, and a detail, a text.Text it
// owns. Both fields are pub, read as f.err and f.detail, and the detail
// is written through its view, as in
//
//     fmt.printf("%s: %s\n", f.err.msg, f.detail.bytes())
//
// A Failure is owned, as the Text it holds is: it moves rather than
// copies, has no zero value, and frees its detail when it goes out of
// scope, a list or a map that holds it included.
//
// A Failure is built as a literal, errors.Failure{err: e, detail: t}, or
// by new, errors.new(e, own t), each of which moves a Text the caller
// built in and cannot fail, or by from_bytes, which copies a view's bytes into a new detail; when the detail
// cannot be made it keeps the error and gives an empty detail, having
// allocated nothing, so a Failure never loses its code for want of
// memory, and from_bytes returns no error of its own. A detail is grown
// afterwards through the field, err := mut f.detail.append(s), all or
// nothing as text's append is.
//
// Nothing here asks what the error is: a Failure of noerror, code 0, is
// a Failure like any other, and the detail may be empty.

// Failure is an error and a message made at run time that goes with it.
pub type Failure struct {
    pub err error
    pub detail text.Text
}

// new returns a Failure of err whose detail is detail, which it takes
// from the caller: the caller writes own before a variable holding it,
// errors.new(e, own t), and cannot use that variable again, and passes a
// Text no variable holds, errors.new(e, text.new(alloc)), as it is. It
// allocates nothing, so it cannot fail.
pub func new(err error, own detail text.Text) Failure {
    return Failure{err: err, detail: detail}
}

// from_bytes returns a Failure of err whose detail is a copy of the bytes
// of s, made through alloc. When alloc cannot give the detail, the
// Failure keeps err and its detail is empty, having allocated nothing:
// the error is what matters, and a caller that must know tests
// f.detail.len().
pub func from_bytes(err error, s []uint8, alloc Allocator) Failure {
    t, e := text.from_bytes(s, alloc)
    if e.code != 0 {
        return Failure{err: err, detail: text.new(alloc)}
    }
    return Failure{err: err, detail: t}
}
